Sec. 90001. Appropriates $46.55 billion to the Commissioner of U.S. Customs and Border Protection for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, for border infrastructure and wall system expenses. The funds cover the construction, installation, or improvement of primary, waterborne, and secondary barriers, as well as access roads. It also includes barrier system attributes like cameras, lights, and sensors, and ground preparation work necessary for CBP operations.

Sec. 90002. Appropriates $11.95763 billion to the Commissioner of U.S. Customs and Border Protection for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, for personnel, vehicles, and facilities. This includes $4.1 billion for hiring and training Border Patrol, Office of Field Operations, and Air and Marine agents, and $2.05263 billion for recruitment, performance, and retention bonuses. Additionally, $855 million is allocated for vehicle repair and acquisition, and $5 billion for facility leases, acquisitions, and improvements, with a restriction prohibiting funds for processing coordinator duties after October 31, 2028.

Sec. 90003. Appropriates $45 billion to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, for single adult alien detention capacity and family residential center capacity. The section defines "family residential center" as a facility for detaining family units of aliens, including children who are not unaccompanied alien children. Detention standards for single adults are set at the discretion of the Secretary of Homeland Security, consistent with applicable law.

Sec. 90004. Appropriates $6.168 billion to the Commissioner of U.S. Customs and Border Protection for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, for border security, technology, and screening initiatives. Funds support nonintrusive inspection equipment with AI capabilities, Air and Marine platform upgrades, border surveillance technologies, and biometric entry/exit systems. It also covers screening persons entering or exiting the U.S., initial screenings of unaccompanied alien children, and combating drug trafficking, while restricting funds for untested autonomous surveillance towers.

Sec. 90005. Appropriates $2.575 billion to FEMA for the State Homeland Security Grant Program and establishes the State Border Security Reinforcement Fund with $10 billion for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2034. The grant program funds include $500 million for unmanned aircraft system threat detection, $625 million for 2026 FIFA World Cup security, $1 billion for 2028 Olympics security, and $450 million for the Operation Stonegarden Grant Program. The new fund provides grants to states and local governments for border wall construction, ground preparation, detection of illicit substances and criminals, and relocation of unlawfully present aliens from small population centers.

Sec. 90006. Appropriates $300 million to the Administrator of FEMA for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to reimburse state and local agencies for extraordinary law enforcement costs associated with protecting presidential residences. Funds are limited to costs incurred on or after July 1, 2024, that exceed typical operations and are directly attributable to Secret Service-requested protection of nongovernmental properties. FEMA may use up to 3 percent of these funds for administrative purposes.

Sec. 90007. Appropriates $10 billion to the Secretary of Homeland Security for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, for the reimbursement of costs incurred in supporting the Department’s mission to safeguard U.S. borders.

Sec. 90101. Enacts the "FEHB Protection Act of 2025," requiring the Office of Personnel Management Director to verify family member eligibility for federal health benefits and conduct a three-year audit of enrollment documents. The Director must develop a process within 180 days to disenroll ineligible individuals and include fraud risk assessments for ineligible enrollees. The section amends 5 U.S.C. 8909 to set aside $66 million from contributions for fiscal year 2026, available until fiscal year 2035, to fund these verification, audit, and disenrollment activities.

Sec. 90102. Appropriates $88 million for fiscal year 2026, available until expended, for the Pandemic Response Accountability Committee to oversee the Coronavirus response and funds provided in this Act. It amends the CARES Act to include this reconciliation act in the list of covered laws for oversight purposes. The section also extends the expiration date of the Pandemic Response Accountability Committee from 2025 to 2034.

Sec. 90103. Appropriates $100 million to the Office of Management and Budget for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to identify budget and accounting efficiencies within the executive branch. [end of text]


